VALUES (4400093533030129, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A practical problem in Data Structure requires the behaviour or method described by the syllabus area concerned with Data Structure. Explain how Data Structure applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Data Structure requires the behaviour or method described by the syllabus area concerned with Linear Data Structure. Explain how Study of Linear Data Structure applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Data Structure requires the behaviour or method described by items must be processed in their arrival order. Explain how Introduction to Queue applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Data Structure requires the behaviour or method described by the syllabus area concerned with Non-linear Data Structure. Explain how Non-linear Data Structure applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Data Structure is covered in the syllabus unit “Introduction to Basics of Data Structure”. A complete answer should define the concept, describe its role and steps/components, relate it to Data Representation, Data Types: Primitive and Non-Primitive, Algorithm, Time Complexity, provide a practical example, and state a relevant check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Data Representation; Data Types: Primitive and Non-Primitive; Algorithm; Time Complexity. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Data Structure task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Study of Linear Data Structure is covered in the syllabus unit “Array & Stack”. A complete answer should define the concept, describe its role and steps/components, relate it to Representation of Array, Two- Dimensional Array, Multidimensional Array, Sparse Matrices, provide a practical example, and state a relevant check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Representation of Array; Two- Dimensional Array; Multidimensional Array; Sparse Matrices. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Data Structure task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** A queue normally uses first-in, first-out ordering. Enqueue adds at the rear and dequeue removes from the front; this is useful for scheduling and buffering.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Queue Operations: Insertion; Deletion; Types of Queue: Simple; Circular. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Data Structure task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Non-linear Data Structure is covered in the syllabus unit “Tree And Graph”. A complete answer should define the concept, describe its role and steps/components, relate it to Introduction to Binary Tree, Tree Terminologies: Tree, Degree of a Node, Degree of a Tree, provide a practical example, and state a relevant check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Introduction to Binary Tree; Tree Terminologies: Tree; Degree of a Node; Degree of a Tree. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Data Structure task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

