VALUES (4400093523020135, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic task involving Introducing the database and DBMS in Database Management System. Explain the governing principle, justify the method or decision, and include one verification check and one limitation."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic task involving Basic Entity Relationship Concepts: Entities in Database Management System. Explain the governing principle, justify the method or decision, and include one verification check and one limitation."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic task involving Introduction to SQL : Data Definition Commands in Database Management System. Explain the governing principle, justify the method or decision, and include one verification check and one limitation."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic task involving Files and File Systems in Database Management System. Explain the governing principle, justify the method or decision, and include one verification check and one limitation."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Introducing the database and DBMS is covered in the syllabus unit “Database Systems Data Models”. A complete answer should define the concept, describe its role and steps/components, relate it to Files and File Systems, Problems with File System and advantages of Database Management systems, The importance of Data models, Data Model Basic Building Blocks, provide a practical example, and state a relevant check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Files and File Systems; Problems with File System and advantages of Database Management systems; The importance of Data models; Data Model Basic Building Blocks. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Database Management System task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** An ER model describes entities, attributes and relationships; cardinality and participation constraints inform conversion into a relational schema.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Relationship; Attributes; E R Diagram symbols; Conversion of Entity Relationship Model into Relations. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Database Management System task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** SQL defines and queries relational data. SELECT retrieves rows, WHERE filters them, JOIN combines related rows and aggregates summarize data.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Data Manipulation Commands; Select queries; Advanced Data Definition Commands; Advanced Select queries. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Database Management System task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Files and File Systems is covered in the syllabus unit “Database Systems Data Models”. A complete answer should define the concept, describe its role and steps/components, relate it to Introducing the database and DBMS, Problems with File System and advantages of Database Management systems, The importance of Data models, Data Model Basic Building Blocks, provide a practical example, and state a relevant check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Introducing the database and DBMS; Problems with File System and advantages of Database Management systems; The importance of Data models; Data Model Basic Building Blocks. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Database Management System task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

