 mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Logic Gates: Positive and Negative logic is covered in the syllabus unit “Module-3 Logic Gates & Boolean Algebra”. A complete answer should define the concept, describe its role and steps/components, relate it to NOT, NAND, NOR, X-OR Gate, provide a practical example, and state a relevant check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as NOT; NAND; NOR; X-OR Gate. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Digital Electronics task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** A flip-flop is a bistable sequential circuit that stores one bit and changes state according to inputs and clocking conditions.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as S-R flip-flops; D flip flop; J-K flip flop; Application of flip flops. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Digital Electronics task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Introduction to Programmable Logic Devices is covered in the syllabus unit “Module-6 Programmable Devices”. A complete answer should define the concept, describe its role and steps/components, relate it to Programmable Logic Arrays (PLA), Programmable Array Logic (PAL)., Module-6 Programmable Devices, provide a practical example, and state a relevant check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Programmable Logic Arrays (PLA); Programmable Array Logic (PAL).; Module-6 Programmable Devices. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Digital Electronics task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

